  {
    "type": "post",
    "id": "7467520842059513857",
    "linkedinUrl": "https://www.linkedin.com/posts/stuart-hardman_learning-to-pass-an-exam-vs-learning-to-do-activity-7467520842059513857-gSqk",
    "content": "Learning to pass an exam vs learning to do a job.\n\nIt's a real issue, for some reason when people set exams they often do it from a very literal \"follow the rules\" way and not from a practical real world experience approach.\n\nThere are certain exams that we take in our industry that show you know your way around Business Central. However, what they don't show is that you know how to deal with customers, question why people are trying to do something, and ensuring you can set the system up in an appropriate way.\n\nI know someone studying for the MB-800 exam and thankfully they've picked up how badly worded the questions are and how they expect you to answer. They've also pointed out that it isn't how they would do it in the real world. That's a relief! The difference between text books (or Learn websites) and real world is huge. \n\nA couple of examples below:\n\nIn the first i'd always be questioning why they're trying to post to the blocked dimensions rather than just ploughing in and unblocking them. It might be legitimate but in my experience a lot of the time it's end user error.\n\nIn the second I honestly had never noticed that the page was called \"Edit Dimension Set Entries\" - you click the Dimensions action to get to it. And then the final point about \"Implementing Codeunit 408\" is bizarre, you don't implement it, it's standard functionality, it's just there and baked in to the system.\n\nIf you have to sit any of these Microsoft exams (or anyone elses) what are your biggest frustrations with them?",
