  {
    "type": "post",
    "id": "7485615263611088896",
    "linkedinUrl": "https://www.linkedin.com/posts/stuart-hardman_businesscentral-dynamics365-erp-activity-7485615263611088896-DePk",
    "content": "\"𝗪𝗵𝗮𝘁 𝘀𝘁𝗼𝗽𝘀 𝗠𝗶𝗰𝗿𝗼𝘀𝗼𝗳𝘁 𝘂𝗽𝗱𝗮𝘁𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗕𝘂𝘀𝗶𝗻𝗲𝘀𝘀 𝗖𝗲𝗻𝘁𝗿𝗮𝗹 𝗶𝗻 𝘁𝗵𝗲 𝗺𝗶𝗱𝗱𝗹𝗲 𝗼𝗳 𝗮 𝗯𝘂𝘀𝘆 𝘄𝗼𝗿𝗸𝗱𝗮𝘆?\"\n\nIt's a question I've been asked quite a few times by customers considering moving to Business Central SaaS.\n\nThe answer is update windows.\n\nFor our customers, we configure maintenance windows so Microsoft knows when updates can be applied with the least disruption to the business.\n\nOne of our customers was due to receive a production update last night. The update started as planned, but something interesting happened.\n\nThe monitoring systems determined that the update wasn't going to complete within the agreed update window. Rather than overrunning into business hours and causing potential disruption, Microsoft automatically stopped the update and rolled everything back.\n\nThis morning the system was fully available and the customer could carry on working as normal.\n\nIt's one of those behind-the-scenes features that most people never see, but it highlights the amount of engineering that goes into running a modern SaaS service.\n\nWhen people think about Business Central SaaS, they often focus on new features, automatic upgrades and not having to manage servers.\n\nFor me, it's also about things like this. The safeguards that make sure updates happen at the right time, in the right way, and don't get in the way of the business.\n\nNot the most glamorous feature, but definitely one of the most important. 👍\n\n#BusinessCentral #Dynamics365 #ERP #MicrosoftDynamics365 #SaaS #CloudERP\n\nA picture of an update window would be really boring so here's one of my favourite window pictures I took on holiday 3 years ago. Feel free to guess where it is.",
